    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a good baseline. However, if you look at how these theories actually play out in court, the Originalist argument seems to rely on a very concrete, historical textual analysis, whereas the Living Constitution argument often feels a bit more abstract or even subjective. Could you expand on the specific legal logic used by Originalists to justify a particular interpretation, and then contrast that with the most common criticisms leveled against the Living Constitution approach?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Originalists justify their approach by demanding \"text and context,\" strictly relying on historical evidence such as the 1791 context of the militia to determine that the individual right is fixed within the specific historical framework established by the Framers. In response, critics of the Living Constitution argue that it is inherently subjective because it requires judges to make discretionary policy choices rather than applying neutral legal texts, effectively turning the court into a legislative branch. This critique highlights the tension between the desire for judicial neutrality and the inevitable influence of the interpreting judge's personal values when the constitutional language is assumed to be open-ended.",
      "source": "target",
